==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|de|Arginin}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἄργυρος||[[silver]]}} and {{m|grc|ἀργινόεις||[[silvery]], [[brightly]] [[shining]]}} in reference to arginine nitrate crystals' silver-white appearance when first refined from lupin seedlings by Ernst Schulze and Ernst Steiger in 1886.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɑɹɡɪniːn/|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɑːdʒɪniːn/|a=UK}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[amino acid]] found in animal foods that plays an important role in several [[physiological]] processes.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=The Curious Man: The Life and Works of Dr. Hans Nieper |author=Hans Nieper | page=67| passage=Since '''arginine''' is a normal component and plays an important role in the glucose transport mechanism, arginates can easily serve as acceptors for glucose. }}

